Growing a file after shrinking it reads zeros where the removed bytes were: when the shrink cut into data the server had not cached, those bytes could reappear once the file grew again, and a write into the shortened part or a second shrink before the next commit could store them in the bucket for good.
Shrinking files no longer builds up memory between commits: each shrink that ended inside a chunk could cost up to a chunk of memory outside -max-dirty, held until or allocated at the next commit, so enough size changes, even on one empty file, could exhaust the server's memory without any data being written.
Reads no longer return data older than a write that had already been acknowledged, or a mix of data from before and after one write: a read that overlapped a commit of the same file could return the file's previous contents, and a read that overlapped a write to it could return part of that write.
Reads no longer return wrong data after a commit fails partway: a later write to the same file could change a chunk the cache was serving, so other files holding that chunk read the changed bytes until the server restarted, and writing to or truncating one of them could store those bytes in the bucket.
The chunk cache now stays within its -cache size; each chunk it kept after a commit was counted at its length but held the whole chunk-size write buffer, so after many small files it could use many times the memory configured.
Buffered write data is bounded by a new -max-dirty flag (MiB, default 256; 0 or less for no limit): once unflushed writes hold that much memory, further writes wait while the server commits, and fail if that commit fails.
Acknowledged data is no longer silently lost; a write that arrived while the periodic commit or a client COMMIT was flushing the file could discard earlier writes to the same chunk, even ones a COMMIT had confirmed.
A FILE_SYNC write returns instead of hanging; the hung write blocked every later COMMIT, periodic commit and shutdown, so nothing further was committed to the bucket.
A DATA_SYNC write is committed to the bucket before the reply and answered FILE_SYNC, instead of being answered UNSTABLE, which RFC 1813 forbids.
A non-idempotent NFS request retransmitted on the same connection gets the original reply instead of being executed a second time; a duplicate request cache remembers recent replies.
Chunks are verified against the hash that names them when fetched from the bucket; a mismatch fails the operation with NFS3ERR_IO. New -verify-chunks flag, default on.
